From ed71e8e057d53aa478770a9adbd8b9426b7def9c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:36:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 084/119] scene-referred S5: the camera's grade

The controls that drive the chain the previous commits built. Four property groups, all live -
the tone map reads plain numbers off the bus and builds the white-balance matrix itself, so there
is no LUT bake to wait for and a dragged slider moves the picture on the next frame.

- "White Balance" gains Tint beside the existing Kelvin. Tint is the other axis - the
  green/magenta correction a temperature alone cannot make, for light off the blackbody locus.
  Positive is magenta. The existing member and its CRC are untouched; every saved level opens
  with the same 6500 K it had.
- "Grade": ASC CDL slope, offset and power as RGB triples, saturation, and Bypass Grade. The
  bypass takes white balance, CDL and look out in one click and leaves the output transform -
  the A/B, with the exposure and the optics unchanged.
- "Look" and "Output" already carry the two .cube slots.

Neutral is exact, not approximate. 6500 K + tint 0 is an identity because the tone map derives
its reference white from the same Planckian locus rather than from a tabulated D65 - source white
equals destination white, so the Bradford matrix is the identity to the last bit. Slope 1 /
offset 0 / power 1 / saturation 1 is an identity by arithmetic. With the Look slot empty the chain
is nothing but ACES 2.0. D10 is therefore a property of the defaults, not a claim about them.

The old channel-gain white balance - which multiplied E3DPARAM_HDR_COLORGRADING_COLOR_BALANCE,
read by the film curve - now stands down on the scene-referred path and hands the global back.
It would have been inert there anyway (the scene-referred branch never reads that constant), but
leaving it writing a global the time of day also owns is noise nobody could trace.

Not done: .cdl (ASC CDL ColorCorrection XML) import/export. It was the stretch goal and it stays
a follow-up - the numbers on the camera are already the ASC's, in the ASC's order, so the file is
a serialisation job and nothing about the pipeline waits on it.

